$PQ=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} | {{field-6}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Here, $P$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix and $Q$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix.  \n\nThere is $1$ column in matrix $P$ and $1$ row in matrix $Q$, so the product is well defined:\n\n$$\n{P}_{3\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{Q}_{\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 2}={PQ}_{3\\times 2}\n$$\n\nComputing the product, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0\\cdot (-4) & 0\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3\\cdot (-4) & 3\\cdot 2 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-4) & (-1)\\cdot 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396025 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c534267d-c67a-576d-a7c7-8f05a39d217f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396027} {:answer/id #uuid "17ab5397-bb38-59f6-9330-b062d867cf08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396028} {:answer/id #uuid "bbc3f4eb-2bd2-548e-adb8-c1548530611b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396029} {:answer/id #uuid "cd31fdfe-ca0b-590b-9300-ed3e228812d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396030} {:answer/id #uuid "abaf4c24-a433-5f0e-9fa5-6f62768c563e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396027} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb12cf5d-8771-503b-99b4-cf0f73c56ea7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396026}] :question/id #uuid "0dc1caab-e8b2-5f4c-af54-cbacb852a075" :question/math-academy-id "q-13635" :question/problem "Find $AB$, if \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, $A$ is $3\\times 1$ matrix and $B$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in matrix $A$ and $1$ row in matrix $B$, so the product is well defined:\n\n$$\n{A}_{3\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 2}={AB}_{3\\times 2}\n$$\n\nComputing the product, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0\\cdot 1 & 0\\cdot 2 \\\\ (-2)\\cdot 1 & (-2)\\cdot 2 \\\\ (-3)\\cdot 1 & (-3)\\cdot 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Column-Vector By a Row-Vector"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1196 :topic/title "Multiplying Matrices"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116102} :activity/id #uuid "18afd5ba-844e-5a0a-b7f3-a142f4f4628b" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116102 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116488 :tutorial/content "Powers of matrices are similar to powers of numbers. If $A$ is a matrix, then $A^n$ is $A$ multiplied by itself $n$ times: \n\n$$\nA^n = \\underbrace{A\\:\\cdot\\: A\\:\\cdot\\: \\ldots \\:\\cdot\\: A}_{\\Large{n\\text{ times}}}\n$$\n\nFor $A$ to be conformable with itself, $A$ needs to be a square matrix. So we can only take powers of square matrices.\n\nTo demonstrate, suppose that\n![](images/81/81537bed0eafc982114b89fc84baeac44f7a75997c89df5a0f89b8b0a00b2da3.svg)\n\nThen we can calculate $A^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot 2 & 3\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\ 2\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 2 & 2\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -4 \\\\ 8 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe say that $A^0 = I,$ the identity matrix of the same dimension as $A.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ee44491b-6b6b-5f87-9b67-ffc9a32397f7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2999 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "fb346f38-313a-573c-b90a-fbbe590dbd2f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116103}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116103 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115229 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82e970fe-cff8-50e3-9b94-67ad97b03da7"} :step/id #uuid "b7e64a68-2c78-5e75-aff4-57e7b0a1d18e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116104}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116104 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116489 :tutorial/content "Now that we know how to compute powers of matrices, we can evaluate polynomials where the input is a square matrix. \n\nFor example, consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(X) = X^2 + 4X + 3.\n$$\n\nNote that, in order to evaluate this polynomial for an input matrix $X,$ we need to interpret any constants as multiples of the identity matrix. So, we interpret the polynomial as\n\n$$\nf(X) = X^2 + 4X + 3I.\n$$\n\nFor example, if ![](images/6a/6abb7cbdef9c5d7b23d8519468b174de369cfe4af4b9f8c4578ce7dc74812f63.svg) then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can evaluate $f(A)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & ={A}^{2}+4A+3I \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}+4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+4+3 & 3+4+0 \\\\ 0+0+0 & 4+8+3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ac80f32c-3973-5ae6-b2de-3dcfd8494ec9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3000 :tutorial/title "Matrix Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "f5d78f09-ffdb-5e92-a5f6-53a783fa07ca" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116105}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116105 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115230 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61443225-bc08-5d36-a603-3a1d7e392a98"} :step/id #uuid "f98ae8b7-d09c-5763-8545-e0528e7956df"}] :activity/title "Powers of Matrices" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115471}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186050446 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115229 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115059 :question/id #uuid "f832e671-b4bd-5d1d-81e7-b215a8ee4e27" :question/math-academy-id "e-4414" :question/problem "Find $A^4$ given that ![](images/e0/e0e7d5ed9b893352d68931ff5d8fa93f0d0c5173e5955a3f5cdc0cb65d3c3c78.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, to find $A^2,$ we multiply $A$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $A^4 = A^2 \\cdot A^2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{4} & ={A}^{2}\\cdot {A}^{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}81 & 0 \\\\ 100 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82e970fe-cff8-50e3-9b94-67ad97b03da7" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534214 :question-generator/id #uuid "531c64e5-3195-5fc7-908c-a759b